1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,949
This video is about lexical analysis, the first phase of a compiler.

2
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:19,821
You learn what tokens and lexemes are, how to tokenize a program by hand, and the maximal munch rule. Then we look at real languages where lexing is hard: FORTRAN, PL/I and C plus plus.

3
00:00:20,271 --> 00:00:26,479
Here is the compiler pipeline from video 1. Five phases, and the data between them.

4
00:00:26,929 --> 00:00:31,451
This video is about phase 1, the lexical analyzer, or lexer.

5
00:00:31,901 --> 00:00:36,275
The lexer reads the input program and writes a stream of tokens.

6
00:00:36,725 --> 00:00:38,901
The parser then reads the tokens.

7
00:00:39,351 --> 00:00:50,508
What does a lexer do? Here is a small program. You see four lines with indentation: if i equals equals j, z equals 0, else z equals 1.

8
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:55,843
But the lexer does not see lines. The input is one string of characters.

9
00:00:56,293 --> 00:00:58,085
Backslash t is a tab.

10
00:00:58,535 --> 00:01:00,519
Backslash n is a newline.

11
00:01:00,969 --> 00:01:08,479
The goal is to partition this string into substrings. Each substring is a lexeme, and its class is a token.

12
00:01:08,929 --> 00:01:14,518
The lines and the indentation exist only as tab and newline bytes in the string.

13
00:01:14,968 --> 00:01:29,176
A program is a sequence of bytes in a file. Some bytes have no glyph: tab, newline and carriage return. So we write them with an escape sequence. Here is the first line of the example in its written form.

14
00:01:29,626 --> 00:01:32,485
Count the characters on the page: 15.

15
00:01:32,935 --> 00:01:40,849
The slide says 16 characters. Our count of the written form gives 15. The byte count is the number that matters.

16
00:01:41,299 --> 00:01:46,355
In the file, the same line is 13 bytes, at positions 0 to 12.

17
00:01:46,805 --> 00:01:48,533
The first byte is a tab.

18
00:01:48,983 --> 00:01:51,010
The last byte is a newline.

19
00:01:51,460 --> 00:02:01,999
An escape sequence is two characters on the page, but one byte in the file. There are two escape sequences, so 15 minus 2 gives 13 bytes.

20
00:02:02,449 --> 00:02:08,315
Printed as blank space, a tab is ambiguous: is it one tab or four spaces?

21
00:02:08,765 --> 00:02:13,928
Windows ends each line with backslash r backslash n, which is two bytes.

22
00:02:14,378 --> 00:02:29,354
Now tokenize the input by hand, as on the slide. Read the input one byte at a time. Write each lexeme, and next to it, its token: whitespace, keyword, identifier, integer, relation or punctuation.

23
00:02:29,804 --> 00:02:31,447
Pause the video and try it.

24
00:02:31,897 --> 00:02:41,518
Here are the first 15 bytes, with their positions 0 to 14. Read from left to right, and take the longest lexeme that is a token.

25
00:02:41,968 --> 00:02:50,501
Byte 0 is a tab. The next byte, i, is not white space. So the lexeme is one tab, and its token is whitespace.

26
00:02:50,951 --> 00:03:02,130
Next, i. It could be an identifier, but it can still grow. i f is also a token. The next byte is a space, so the lexeme stops. i f is a keyword.

27
00:03:02,580 --> 00:03:05,161
Byte 3 is a space: whitespace.

28
00:03:05,611 --> 00:03:09,131
Byte 4, the open parenthesis, is punctuation.

29
00:03:09,581 --> 00:03:15,107
Byte 5, i, is an identifier. It cannot grow, because a space follows.

30
00:03:15,557 --> 00:03:18,181
Byte 6 is a space: whitespace.

31
00:03:18,631 --> 00:03:31,601
Then equals. One equals sign is a token, but it can grow: equals equals is a relation. The next byte is a space. So the lexeme is equals equals, and the token is relation.

32
00:03:32,051 --> 00:03:34,697
Byte 9 is a space: whitespace.

33
00:03:35,147 --> 00:03:37,685
Byte 10, j, is an identifier.

34
00:03:38,135 --> 00:03:41,421
Byte 11, the close parenthesis, is punctuation.

35
00:03:41,871 --> 00:03:52,324
Last, the newline and the two tabs. All three are white space, so they make one lexeme: whitespace. These are the 11 rows of the slide answer.

36
00:03:52,774 --> 00:03:58,022
Here is the whole input: 36 bytes, at positions 0 to 35.

37
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:05,981
Continue the same way to the end. The bars show the lexemes, coloured by token. There are 26 lexemes.

38
00:04:06,431 --> 00:04:16,053
15 of them are tokens for the parser: 2 keywords, 4 identifiers, 2 integers, 1 relation and 6 punctuation marks.

39
00:04:16,503 --> 00:04:21,857
The other 11 lexemes are whitespace. 15 plus 11 is 26.

40
00:04:22,307 --> 00:04:32,185
The lexer usually drops the whitespace. So the parser gets 15 tokens: if, open parenthesis, i, equals equals, j, and so on.

41
00:04:32,635 --> 00:04:40,123
Each token carries its lexeme. For example, keyword with lexeme if, and identifier with lexeme i.

42
00:04:40,573 --> 00:04:49,106
What is a token? A token is a syntactic category. In English, the categories are noun, verb, adjective, and so on.

43
00:04:49,556 --> 00:04:56,532
In a programming language, the categories are identifier, integer, keyword, whitespace, and so on.

44
00:04:56,982 --> 00:05:01,761
So a token is a category, and a lexeme is one string in that category.

45
00:05:02,211 --> 00:05:06,456
For example, i is a lexeme. Identifier is its token.

46
00:05:06,906 --> 00:05:13,925
Each token is a set of strings. Identifier: strings of letters or digits that start with a letter.

47
00:05:14,375 --> 00:05:17,639
Keyword: else, or if, or begin, and so on.

48
00:05:18,089 --> 00:05:20,841
Integer: a non-empty string of digits.

49
00:05:21,291 --> 00:05:25,792
Whitespace: a non-empty sequence of blanks, newlines and tabs.

50
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:30,039
Relation: equals equals, less than, less than or equal.

51
00:05:30,489 --> 00:05:34,201
And each punctuation mark is a token of its own.

52
00:05:34,651 --> 00:05:47,985
There is a problem. The string if is in two sets. It is a keyword, and it also matches the identifier rule. The slide answer makes if a keyword. So a keyword wins over an identifier.

53
00:05:48,435 --> 00:05:50,248
The same is true for else.

54
00:05:50,698 --> 00:05:57,077
A quick check. For each string, which token set contains it? Try them before the answers appear.

55
00:05:57,527 --> 00:06:01,089
z 1 starts with a letter, so it is an identifier.

56
00:06:01,539 --> 00:06:06,446
0 0 7 is a non-empty string of digits: an integer.

57
00:06:06,896 --> 00:06:09,499
A newline and a tab are white space.

58
00:06:09,949 --> 00:06:12,253
Less than or equal is a relation.

59
00:06:12,703 --> 00:06:21,577
But 1 z is in no set. It starts with a digit, so it is not an identifier. It has a letter, so it is not an integer.

60
00:06:22,027 --> 00:06:27,574
A lexer cuts it into two lexemes: the integer 1, then the identifier z.

61
00:06:28,024 --> 00:06:36,301
What are tokens for? They classify substrings of the program by their role. The output of the lexer is a stream of tokens.

62
00:06:36,751 --> 00:06:39,162
That stream is the input of the parser.

63
00:06:39,612 --> 00:06:47,057
The parser relies on the token distinctions. For example, it treats an identifier differently from a keyword.

64
00:06:47,507 --> 00:07:07,027
To design a lexer, step 1: define a finite set of tokens. The tokens describe all items of interest. The choice depends on the language and on the design of the parser. For our example: integer, keyword, relation, identifier, whitespace, and four single tokens.

65
00:07:07,477 --> 00:07:14,197
Here open parenthesis, close parenthesis, equals and semicolon are tokens, not characters.

66
00:07:14,647 --> 00:07:23,757
Step 2: describe which strings belong to each token. The exact way to do this is a regular expression. That is the next video.

67
00:07:24,207 --> 00:07:27,833
How is a lexer implemented? It must do two things.

68
00:07:28,283 --> 00:07:37,115
First, recognize the substrings that correspond to tokens. For example, it recognizes that i f is in the keyword set.

69
00:07:37,565 --> 00:07:44,477
Second, return the token and its value, the lexeme. The lexeme is the substring that was tokenized.

70
00:07:44,927 --> 00:07:49,855
For example, keyword with lexeme if, and identifier with lexeme i.

71
00:07:50,305 --> 00:07:59,244
The lexer usually discards uninteresting tokens, which do not contribute to parsing. Examples are white space and comments.

72
00:07:59,694 --> 00:08:08,355
Is lexing as easy as it sounds? Not quite. Look at some history. In FORTRAN, the rule is: whitespace is insignificant.

73
00:08:08,805 --> 00:08:17,915
So V A, three spaces, R 1 is the same as V A R 1. The lexer removes the blanks, and both give one identifier.

74
00:08:18,365 --> 00:08:20,989
This means a blank cannot end a token.

75
00:08:21,439 --> 00:08:27,732
The rule came from punch cards: the operators who typed the cards were not accurate with spaces.

76
00:08:28,182 --> 00:08:30,678
The slides call it a terrible design.

77
00:08:31,128 --> 00:08:46,744
This example is optional. It is not on the slides, but it shows why the FORTRAN rule is a problem. Take the line D O 5 I equals 1 comma 25. With the blanks removed, the lexer sees these characters.

78
00:08:47,194 --> 00:08:55,514
Now take D O 5 I equals 1 point 25. Only one character is different: a point instead of a comma.

79
00:08:55,964 --> 00:09:01,553
The first line is a loop: the keyword D O, the label 5, and the variable I.

80
00:09:02,003 --> 00:09:06,761
The second line is an assignment to one variable called D O 5 I.

81
00:09:07,211 --> 00:09:15,659
The first six characters are the same. To decide if D O is a keyword, the lexer must look ahead to the comma or the point.

82
00:09:16,109 --> 00:09:32,941
The slides make two points. First, the lexer reads left to right and recognizes one token at a time. Second, lookahead may be needed to decide where one token ends and the next token begins. Even our simple example has this issue.

83
00:09:33,391 --> 00:09:39,087
Start at i. i is an identifier. But it may be the start of if, so keep going.

84
00:09:39,537 --> 00:09:43,633
i f is a keyword, and a longer token may still follow.

85
00:09:44,083 --> 00:09:50,888
The next character is a space. i f space is not a token. So stop, and emit the keyword if.

86
00:09:51,338 --> 00:09:59,018
Later, the lexer reaches an equals sign. One equals is a token, but it may be the start of equals equals.

87
00:09:59,468 --> 00:10:01,943
Equals equals is a relation.

88
00:10:02,393 --> 00:10:07,833
Equals equals space is not a token. So emit the relation equals equals.

89
00:10:08,283 --> 00:10:19,525
This is the rule of maximal munch, or longest match. Keep extending the current token while it can still grow. Emit it when the next character cannot extend it.

90
00:10:19,975 --> 00:10:26,098
Here, one character of lookahead decides: i or if, and equals or equals equals.

91
00:10:26,548 --> 00:10:38,900
Now an exercise. Tokenize i f 1 less than or equal 2 0 semicolon. Use maximal munch and the tokens of the slides. Give each lexeme and its token.

92
00:10:39,350 --> 00:10:40,993
Pause the video and try it.

93
00:10:41,443 --> 00:10:45,496
Start at byte 0. Grow the lexeme while it is still a token.

94
00:10:45,946 --> 00:10:47,781
i is an identifier.

95
00:10:48,231 --> 00:10:49,895
i f is a keyword.

96
00:10:50,345 --> 00:10:55,635
i f 1 is an identifier: letters and digits that start with a letter.

97
00:10:56,085 --> 00:11:02,016
i f 1 less than is not a token. So emit i f 1 as one identifier.

98
00:11:02,466 --> 00:11:05,517
Next, at byte 3: less than is a relation.

99
00:11:05,967 --> 00:11:08,889
Less than or equal is also a relation.

100
00:11:09,339 --> 00:11:14,971
Less than or equal 2 is not a token. Emit the relation less than or equal.

101
00:11:15,421 --> 00:11:18,472
Next, at byte 5: 2 is an integer.

102
00:11:18,922 --> 00:11:21,226
2 0 is also an integer.

103
00:11:21,676 --> 00:11:26,561
2 0 semicolon is not a token. Emit the integer 2 0.

104
00:11:27,011 --> 00:11:33,475
Last, the semicolon at byte 7 is punctuation. It is the end of the input, so emit it.

105
00:11:33,925 --> 00:11:44,315
So there are four tokens: the identifier i f 1, the relation less than or equal, the integer 2 0, and the punctuation semicolon.

106
00:11:44,765 --> 00:11:55,047
A common error is to take the keyword if and then the integer 1. Maximal munch takes the longest match, so i f 1 is one identifier.

107
00:11:55,497 --> 00:12:09,855
In PL/I, keywords are not reserved. You may use a keyword as a variable name. Here is a legal statement: IF ELSE THEN THEN equals ELSE semicolon, ELSE ELSE equals THEN. Which words are keywords?

108
00:12:10,305 --> 00:12:12,545
Pause the video and find the keywords.

109
00:12:12,995 --> 00:12:17,603
The statement has the form: IF condition THEN statement ELSE statement.

110
00:12:18,053 --> 00:12:20,293
So the first IF is a keyword.

111
00:12:20,743 --> 00:12:24,113
The next word, ELSE, is the condition: a variable.

112
00:12:24,563 --> 00:12:26,270
Then THEN is a keyword.

113
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:31,648
THEN equals ELSE is an assignment. Here THEN and ELSE are both variables.

114
00:12:32,098 --> 00:12:34,850
After the semicolon, ELSE is a keyword.

115
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:39,631
Then ELSE equals THEN is another assignment of two variables.

116
00:12:40,081 --> 00:12:47,931
So only 3 of the 8 words are keywords. The same word has different tokens, and only the context decides.

117
00:12:48,381 --> 00:12:57,128
Another PL/I problem is the declaration: DECLARE, open parenthesis, ARG 1 to ARG N, close parenthesis.

118
00:12:57,578 --> 00:13:04,853
The lexer cannot tell whether DECLARE is a keyword or an array reference until after the close parenthesis.

119
00:13:05,303 --> 00:13:08,545
If it is a declaration, DECLARE is a keyword.

120
00:13:08,995 --> 00:13:12,729
If it is an array reference, DECLARE is an identifier.

121
00:13:13,179 --> 00:13:17,445
N can be any number, so the lexer needs arbitrary lookahead.

122
00:13:17,895 --> 00:13:24,637
The problems continue today, in C plus plus. A template uses angle brackets: Foo of Bar.

123
00:13:25,087 --> 00:13:30,527
And greater than greater than is the right shift operator: 8 shifted right by 1.

124
00:13:30,977 --> 00:13:42,454
Now nest two templates: Foo of Bar of Bazz, with two closing brackets. Maximal munch takes the longest match. So the lexer makes one token, the shift operator.

125
00:13:42,904 --> 00:13:50,563
But the parser needs two closing brackets, one for each template. This is a conflict between the lexer and the grammar.

126
00:13:51,013 --> 00:14:01,381
Before C plus plus 11, the workaround was an extra space between the two brackets. The space stops the munch, so the lexer makes two tokens.

127
00:14:01,831 --> 00:14:09,809
We checked this with g plus plus. Without the space, the old standard gives an error. With the space, the code compiles.

128
00:14:10,259 --> 00:14:16,681
C plus plus 11 fixed the language, not the lexer. The lexer still makes one shift token.

129
00:14:17,131 --> 00:14:23,573
The parser splits the shift token into two closing brackets when it is inside template arguments.

130
00:14:24,023 --> 00:14:26,989
The first closing bracket closes Bar of Bazz.

131
00:14:27,439 --> 00:14:29,017
The second closes Foo.

132
00:14:29,467 --> 00:14:39,878
What if you want a real shift inside a template argument? Use parentheses: Value of, open parenthesis, 8 shift 1, close parenthesis.

133
00:14:40,328 --> 00:14:53,213
8 is 1 0 0 0 in binary. Shifted right by 1, it is 0 1 0 0, which is 4. So this is Value of 4. We compiled and ran this to check it.

134
00:14:53,663 --> 00:15:05,034
The rule is in the C plus plus 11 standard, section 14.2. Inside template arguments, the first non-nested shift token splits into two.

135
00:15:05,484 --> 00:15:12,204
To summarize. The input of the lexer is a string of bytes. A tab and a newline are one byte each.

136
00:15:12,654 --> 00:15:21,102
The goal of lexical analysis is to partition the input string into lexemes, and to identify the token of each lexeme.

137
00:15:21,552 --> 00:15:30,171
A token is a set of strings. A keyword wins over an identifier. The lexer usually discards white space and comments.

138
00:15:30,621 --> 00:15:39,965
The lexer scans left to right, one token at a time. With maximal munch, it takes the longest match, so lookahead is sometimes needed.

139
00:15:40,415 --> 00:15:49,716
Real languages make this hard: blanks in FORTRAN, keywords and DECLARE in PL/I, and the shift token in C plus plus.

140
00:15:50,166 --> 00:15:55,542
Next video: regular expressions, the exact way to describe each token.
